# tractorsupply-scraper — credentials and defaults
#
# Copy this file to `.env` next to the scripts and fill in what you use:
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# `.env` is in .gitignore. Keep it there. A secret passed on a command line
# is readable by anything that can run `ps` and lands in your shell history,
# which is why none of these has a flag you are expected to type.
#
# Precedence, highest first:
#
#     explicit CLI flag  >  exported environment variable  >  .env  >  default
#
# Check what was picked up, without printing any secret:
#
#     python3 env_config.py
#
# Every variable below is one this code actually reads, and every variable
# the code reads is below. smoke_test.py asserts the two sets are equal in
# both directions.
#
# READ THIS FIRST: on this site you need the Scraping Browser endpoint.
#
# Measured 2026-09-28, Akamai refused every client this repo tried except
# one: plain HTTP, local Chromium headless and headful, from a datacentre and
# from a US residential proxy, and 2Captcha's Scraper API were all answered
# with "Access Denied". The 2Captcha Scraping Browser with a US exit was
# served. See the README's access table.


# --- 2Captcha -------------------------------------------------------------
# One key, four separately-billed products: captcha solving, the Scraping
# Browser API, proxies and fingerprints. https://2captcha.com
#
# This repo solves no captcha: none of the refusals the site served carried
# one. The key is what --fingerprint uses, which is a separate subscription.
TWOCAPTCHA_KEY=your_2captcha_api_key_here

# --- Scraping Browser API -------------------------------------------------
# A remote browser you do not run or patch, with a persistent profile and a
# chosen exit country. Used by --cdp-endpoint, and on this site it is the
# one client that was served.
#
#   country-  us. The site sells in the United States only, and every store
#             a run can price against is a US store.
#   pid-      a profile with persistent cookies. ONE live connection each,
#             capped per account. Reuse pids rather than minting one per
#             run, and expect a 500 (`profile_locked`, exit 5) while another
#             run still holds this one.
#
# Selenium cannot use this: chromedriver's `debuggerAddress` takes a bare
# host:port with nowhere to put a password. Playwright and pyppeteer can.
#
# The credentials in a profile expire (measured from about a day to more than ten days), so never paste a
# working one anywhere that is committed.
TRACTORSUPPLY_CDP_ENDPOINT=ws://{login}-zone-scraping_browser-country-us-pid-{profileId}:{password}@cb.2captcha.com:9222

# --- Proxy ----------------------------------------------------------------
# One URL here, or --proxy-file with one per line for a pool. Read by the
# engines that launch their own browser.
#
# Honest status: a US residential exit was NOT enough on 2026-09-28. Local
# Chromium through one was refused (headful) or had its connection reset
# (headless). It is here for parity, and because a different exit or a
# different day may answer differently; measure before you pay for volume.
#
# The credentials never reach the browser's command line: they go through
# the driver's own fields, and logs mask them while keeping host and port.
# Selenium's --proxy-server cannot authenticate at all, so that engine
# strips the credentials and warns.
TRACTORSUPPLY_PROXY=http://{user}:{password}@na.proxy.2captcha.com:2334

# --- Default target -------------------------------------------------------
# Saves retyping --url. A listing on the site:
#
#   https://www.tractorsupply.com/tsc/catalog/poultry-feed-treats   category
#   https://www.tractorsupply.com/tsc/category/pet                  department
#   https://www.tractorsupply.com/tsc/search/dog%20food             search
#
# A --category or --search typed on the command line wins over it.
TRACTORSUPPLY_URL=https://www.tractorsupply.com/tsc/catalog/poultry-feed-treats

# Deliberately NOT here: an output prefix or a ZIP. `--out` carries a
# non-empty default, so the loader, which only fills UNSET values, would
# never see a variable for it. The ZIP is part of the QUERY (prices are per
# store), and a query setting hidden in a file is how two runs end up
# priced at two different stores without anyone noticing.
